item.text:
I searched official budget execution records, UN expert reports, presidential/state-of-siege remedies, military accountability, and independent implementation assessments. The strongest additions are disaggregated defence expenditure rows, independently observed but mostly preparatory DDR implementation, a controversial prisoner release, and contrary evidence showing failure to investigate police abuses.

Unresolved: no reliable 2022 procurement-contract register, off-budget defence ledger, payroll audit identifying ghost soldiers, or named presidential beneficiary/patronage network was accessible. I also found no presidentially ordered 2022 investigation or victim remedy for state-of-siege abuses. The SIPRI country-row extraction could not be recovered through the accessible interface; the official Congolese execution statement is more granular but not methodologically interchangeable with SIPRI. The searches increasingly repeated the existing UN findings on FARDC proxy cooperation and Tshisekedi’s May corrective instruction.

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"ESB de dépenses par administration et rubrique : exécution au 30/11/2022 (situation provisoire)","publisher":"Ministère du Budget de la République démocratique du Congo","publication_date":"2023-01-25","url":"https://budget.gouv.cd/wp-content/uploads/execution/esb2022/novembre/global/esb_global_par_administration_rubrique.pdf","claim":"The official provisional execution statement recorded CDF 1.181 trillion in voted credits for Defence and Veterans but CDF 1.776 trillion in payment orders by 30 November 2022, about 150 percent of the original appropriation. The record establishes a large aggregate defence overrun but does not identify an off-budget mechanism, improper beneficiary, patronage network, or presidential direction.","locator":"PDF p. 3, administration 27 “Défense et anciens combattants,” aggregate row: Crédit voté 1,181,079,952,530; Ordonnancement 1,775,752,363,699; document header identifies execution through 30 November 2022 and provisional status","provisional_id":"WEB-2B-C2-001","canonical_fact_key":"drc_defence_aggregate_budget_overrun_2022","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["2B"],"methodology_ids":["2B.8"],"source_type":"official_budget_execution_record","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary Ministry of Budget execution statement with a dated, administration-specific row; provisional status and absence of an audit finding limit interpretation.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct measurement of January-November 2022 execution, published shortly after the period.","ruler_attribution":"National executive fiscal context under Tshisekedi; the row does not attribute the overrun, authorization, or benefit to him personally.","contrary_evidence":"The statement alone does not establish corruption or off-budget spending and may include lawful wartime reallocations or supplementary execution.","underlying_fact_key":"drc_defence_aggregate_budget_overrun_2022","lenses":["2B.8"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"ESB de dépenses par administration et rubrique : exécution au 30/11/2022 (situation provisoire) — defence operating expenditure","publisher":"Ministère du Budget de la République démocratique du Congo","publication_date":"2023-01-25","url":"https://budget.gouv.cd/wp-content/uploads/execution/esb2022/novembre/global/esb_global_par_administration_rubrique.pdf","claim":"Within the defence administration, ministry operating expenditure had CDF 339.619 billion voted but CDF 1.271 trillion ordered for payment by 30 November 2022, nearly four times the appropriation. This sharply localizes the aggregate overrun to operating expenditure, while the document provides no procurement objects, suppliers, beneficiaries, or legality finding.","locator":"PDF p. 3, administration 27, row 1 “Fonctionnement des Ministères”: Crédit voté 339,618,936,376; Ordonnancement 1,270,603,993,293; Transfert 516,355,705,758","provisional_id":"WEB-2B-C2-002","canonical_fact_key":"drc_defence_operating_expenditure_overrun_2022","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["2B"],"methodology_ids":["2B.8"],"source_type":"official_budget_execution_record","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary, disaggregated Ministry of Budget execution row; it measures fiscal execution but is not a procurement or forensic audit.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct January-November 2022 defence-execution data.","ruler_attribution":"Executive-branch defence spending during Tshisekedi’s presidency, without evidence that he personally ordered the excess or selected its recipients.","contrary_evidence":"No supplier-level data or adverse audit conclusion establishes diversion, patronage, or illegality.","underlying_fact_key":"drc_defence_operating_expenditure_overrun_2022","lenses":["2B.8"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"ESB de dépenses par administration et rubrique : exécution au 30/11/2022 (situation provisoire) — defence payroll","publisher":"Ministère du Budget de la République démocratique du Congo","publication_date":"2023-01-25","url":"https://budget.gouv.cd/wp-content/uploads/execution/esb2022/novembre/global/esb_global_par_administration_rubrique.pdf","claim":"The defence remuneration row shows CDF 483.767 billion voted and CDF 453.189 billion ordered for payment through November 2022, approximately 93.7 percent execution. This recovers a defence-specific payroll aggregate but supplies no headcount, unit roster, ghost-soldier finding, arrears analysis, or named beneficiary.","locator":"PDF p. 3, administration 27, row 5 “Rémunérations”: Crédit voté 483,766,935,053; Ordonnancement 453,189,163,376; Transfert 453,154,811,396","provisional_id":"WEB-2B-C2-003","canonical_fact_key":"drc_defence_payroll_execution_2022","disposition":"context","chapter_ids":["2B"],"methodology_ids":["2B.8"],"source_type":"official_budget_execution_record","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary defence-specific payroll totals with a stable row locator, but no payroll audit or personnel-level detail.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Direct January-November 2022 payroll-execution data.","ruler_attribution":"National defence payroll under Tshisekedi’s executive administration; no personal presidential beneficiary or intervention is identified.","contrary_evidence":"The near-appropriation execution rate does not itself indicate payroll abuse, and the record cannot test ghost personnel or diversion.","underlying_fact_key":"drc_defence_payroll_execution_2022","lenses":["2B.8"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"ESB de dépenses par administration et rubrique : exécution au 30/11/2022 (situation provisoire) — domestically financed defence investment","publisher":"Ministère du Budget de la République démocratique du Congo","publication_date":"2023-01-25","url":"https://budget.gouv.cd/wp-content/uploads/execution/esb2022/novembre/global/esb_global_par_administration_rubrique.pdf","claim":"For defence investment financed from domestic resources, CDF 350 billion was voted but only CDF 42.604 billion was ordered for payment and CDF 18.438 billion transferred by 30 November 2022. The row indicates low recorded execution of this procurement/investment envelope, not the identity of purchased equipment, contractors, or spending outside the budget.","locator":"PDF p. 3, administration 27, row 4 “Investissements sur Ressources Propres”: Crédit voté 350,000,000,000; Ordonnancement 42,604,029,975; Transfert 18,437,873,256","provisional_id":"WEB-2B-C2-004","canonical_fact_key":"drc_defence_domestic_investment_execution_2022","disposition":"context","chapter_ids":["2B"],"methodology_ids":["2B.8"],"source_type":"official_budget_execution_record","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary appropriation and execution figures with a precise defence row, but no contract-level or off-budget coverage.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Direct January-November 2022 execution data.","ruler_attribution":"National defence-investment context during Tshisekedi’s presidency; no personal procurement decision or beneficiary is established.","contrary_evidence":"Low recorded execution could reflect delayed procurement, classification elsewhere, or non-execution; it cannot prove undisclosed expenditure.","underlying_fact_key":"drc_defence_domestic_investment_execution_2022","lenses":["2B.8"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"Final report of the Group of Experts on the Democratic Republic of the Congo, S/2022/967 — P-DDRCS consultations and implementation limits","publisher":"United Nations Security Council","publication_date":"2022-12-16","url":"https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/3998599/files/S_2022_967-EN.pdf","claim":"A joint Kenyan-DRC team met armed groups in North Kivu, South Kivu, and Ituri in May-June 2022; DRC authorities said 50-60 groups were willing to join P-DDRCS. The programme began creating provincial and local clusters and appointing coordinators, but armed-group participants described slow progress concentrated on logistical capacity, and renewed mobilization against M23 threatened earlier disarmament commitments. This independently verifies consultations and administrative implementation, but not completed demobilization or reintegration outcomes.","locator":"S/2022/967, annex 52, PDF pp. 173-174; especially paragraphs beginning “A joint team composed of representatives” and “Since the Group’s last report”; footnotes 119-121","provisional_id":"WEB-2B-C2-005","canonical_fact_key":"pddrcs_2022_consultations_clusters_limited_implementation","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["2B"],"methodology_ids":["2B.7"],"source_type":"un_expert_investigative_report","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"UN expert report based on government, FARDC, civil-society, and programme sources, explicitly distinguishing claimed adherence from observed implementation constraints.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"All described consultations, appointments, and remobilization occurred during 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Implementation by the DRC government and its P-DDRCS institutions under Tshisekedi; the report does not attribute individual operational steps to him.","contrary_evidence":"Reported willingness to join was supplied partly by DRC authorities; the experts observed slow operational progress and possible reversal of commitments.","underlying_fact_key":"pddrcs_2022_consultations_clusters_limited_implementation","lenses":["2B.7"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"Final report of the Group of Experts on the Democratic Republic of the Congo, S/2022/479 — Ituri Task Force and prisoner release","publisher":"United Nations Security Council","publication_date":"2022-06-14","url":"https://documents.un.org/doc/undoc/gen/n22/338/71/pdf/n2233871.pdf","claim":"A Task Force personally mandated by Tshisekedi to engage Ituri armed groups was taken hostage by CODECO in February 2022. During negotiations for its release, DRC authorities released 59 prisoners suspected of CODECO affiliation; remaining Task Force members were freed after FARDC intervention in April. The release is verified implementation of a prisoner-release measure, but it arose under armed-group coercion and sits uneasily with P-DDRCS rejection of conditionality and amnesty for grave crimes.","locator":"S/2022/479, annex 48, PDF pp. 178-180; paragraphs beginning “In December 2021, the Task Force” and “Furthermore, on 16 February 2022”","provisional_id":"WEB-2B-C2-006","canonical_fact_key":"tshisekedi_ituri_taskforce_codeco_prisoner_release_2022","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["2B"],"methodology_ids":["2B.7"],"source_type":"un_expert_investigative_report","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"UN expert finding with dates, number released, institutional actors, and the presidential mandate identified; the prisoners are described as suspects, not convicted perpetrators.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"The hostage-taking, prisoner release, and FARDC intervention occurred in February-April 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Tshisekedi personally mandated the Task Force, but the report attributes the 59 releases to DRC authorities and does not state that he ordered them.","contrary_evidence":"The release was negotiated amid hostage coercion; the report also questions Task Force leaders’ credibility and stresses that P-DDRCS rejects conditionality and grave-crime amnesties.","underlying_fact_key":"tshisekedi_ituri_taskforce_codeco_prisoner_release_2022","lenses":["2B.7"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"DR Congo: No Inquiry into Police Role in Ethnic Killings","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2022-05-23","url":"https://www.hrw.org/news/2022/05/23/dr-congo-no-inquiry-police-role-ethnic-killings","claim":"After parliamentary inquiries raised possible police involvement in 2021 Buhene killings, burning, and looting, parliament did not establish the recommended inquiry. Tshisekedi’s state of siege suspended the provincial assembly, and he appointed the police commander responsible for the area during the violence as deputy territorial administrator. This is contrary evidence of absent accountability and an affirmative presidential appointment despite unresolved allegations; it is not a finding that the commander personally committed the abuses.","locator":"Sections discussing the provincial and national parliamentary commissions and presidential appointment; web text lines 620-621 and 652-654","provisional_id":"WEB-2B-C2-007","canonical_fact_key":"buhene_police_abuse_no_inquiry_presidential_appointment","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["2B"],"methodology_ids":["2B.9"],"source_type":"independent_human_rights_investigation","source_confidence":"medium","source_confidence_reason":"Detailed independent reporting based on an unpublished parliamentary report and resident testimony; the inquiry failure and appointment are documented, but underlying police complicity remained an allegation rather than an adjudicated finding.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"The accountability failure and continued appointment were specifically documented as persisting in May 2022, although the underlying violence occurred in 2021.","ruler_attribution":"Direct for Tshisekedi’s suspension of provincial parliamentary activity through the state of siege and his appointment decision; no personal role in the underlying violence is alleged.","contrary_evidence":"The former governor’s deployment reportedly assisted wounded residents and removed bodies; no judicial or completed investigative finding established the commander’s individual culpability.","underlying_fact_key":"buhene_police_abuse_no_inquiry_presidential_appointment","lenses":["2B.9"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"En un an d’état de siège, le niveau de violence a augmenté au Nord-Kivu et en Ituri","publisher":"Congo Research Group / Groupe d’étude sur le Congo","publication_date":"2022-05-05","url":"https://www.congoresearchgroup.org/fr/2022/05/05/en-un-an-detat-de-siege-le-niveau-de-violence-a-augmente-au-nord-kivu-et-en-ituri%EF%BF%BC/","claim":"On 28 March 2022, jurisdiction over some civilian cases was restored to civilian courts after roughly nine months, partially correcting the state-of-siege transfer of jurisdiction. The remedy remained narrow because military courts retained exclusive jurisdiction over serious offences including homicide, kidnapping, aggravated theft, and criminal association.","locator":"Section “Des tribunaux débordés,” paragraphs immediately following the heading; web lines 41-45","provisional_id":"WEB-2B-C2-008","canonical_fact_key":"state_of_siege_partial_restoration_civilian_jurisdiction_2022","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["2B"],"methodology_ids":["2B.9"],"source_type":"independent_research_analysis","source_confidence":"medium","source_confidence_reason":"Reputable specialist research group identifies the date, legal change, and retained military jurisdiction, but the underlying presidential or ministerial legal instrument was not recovered.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"The partial jurisdictional correction took effect on 28 March 2022.","ruler_attribution":"A correction by the state-of-siege administration created and maintained under Tshisekedi; the accessible source does not establish that he personally initiated the March measure.","contrary_evidence":"The correction was delayed and partial, leaving civilians accused of major offences under exclusive military jurisdiction and providing no victim investigation or remedy.","underlying_fact_key":"state_of_siege_partial_restoration_civilian_jurisdiction_2022","lenses":["2B.9"]}
